The effect of NaOH and LHW pretreatments on pinewood structure was also investigated by FTIR analysis (Fig. 1
                         and Table 2
                        ). This analysis is mainly used to determine the changes in the crystalline structure and surface functional groups caused by different pretreatments on lignocelluloses. The peak at 898 cm−1, which is corresponding to the cellulose structure, decreased after pretreatment. This confims partial removal of cellulose due to the pretreatment with LHW and NaOH. Complete removal of the peak at 1725 cm−1 was observed after NaOH pretreatment, suggesting the removal of hemicellulose and lignin linkages. The crystallinity index is defined as the ratio of the absorbance at 1098 cm−1 to the absorbance at 898 cm−1, which represents cellulose type I and cellulose type II, respectively. The crystallinity index of untreated sample was 2.33, which decreased to 1.93 and 2.11 after NaOH and LHW pretreatments, respectively. The total crystallinity index was also calculated from the ratio of absorbance at the wavelength of 1377 cm−1 to the absorbance at 2922 cm−1. The total crystallinity index values for untreated, NaOH-pretreated, and LHW-pretreated samples were 1.05, 1.17, and 1.16, respectively.

The yields of methane produced during anaerobic digestion of untreated and pretreated pinewood samples were determined and are illustrated in Fig. 3
                        . The highest methane yield of 57.7 mL/g VS of pretreated pinewood was obtained using the combined pretreatments, including sodium hydroxide, aerobic digestion, and ultrasonic pretreatments. The effectiveness of the biological pretreatment (aerobic digestion) in increasing the methane yield (by 7.3 folds) was considerably more than those of sodium hydroxide and liquid hot water pretreatments, and even more than that of ultrasonic pretreatment. At the first look, it seems that all the applied pretreatments, including biological, physical, chemical, and physicochemical pretreatments, improved the methane production yield. However, statistical analysis revealed that the individual effect of NaOH, LHW, and ultrasonic pretreatments was not significant (P < 0.05) on the improvement of methane yield. The methane contents in the produced biogas (Table 4
                        ) were in the range of 59.1–70.3%. The combination of biological pretreatment with other pretreatments considerably improved the biogas quality in all cases.
